    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-old lady presents with an acute, rapidly progressive, and highly severe redness of the left eye of 2-day duration. The patient reports a massive, continuous, ''gushing'' amount of thick, purulent discharge from the eye that returns immediately after wiping. Physical examination reveals severe eyelid edema, chemosis, marked conjunctival injection, and preauricular lymphadenopathy. Fluorescein staining reveals a peripheral corneal thinning and early ulceration. Gram stain of the discharge shows Gram-negative intracellular diplococci. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Oral acyclovir and tight eye patching for 48 hours", "Urgent enucleation of the left eye to prevent sympathetic ophthalmia", "Topical prednisolone acetate drops every hour without systemic antibiotics", "Intramuscular ceftriaxone (1g) plus oral azithromycin, combined with frequent topical saline irrigation and topical fluoroquinolone drops", "Topical erythromycin ointment monotherapy for 7 days"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient is presenting with **hyperacute bacterial conjunctivitis** caused by **Neisseria gonorrhoeae**. This is a true ophthalmic emergency because N. gonorrhoeae can rapidly penetrate an intact corneal epithelium, leading to corneal ulceration, stromal melting, and globe perforation within 24-48 hours. The clinical hallmark is a hyperacute onset of massive, copious, **''gushing'' purulent discharge** accompanied by severe chemosis and eyelid swelling. Management requires systemic therapy with a single dose of **intramuscular ceftriaxone (1g)** plus oral azithromycin (to cover co-infection with Chlamydia and enhance gonococcicide activity), alongside frequent **topical saline irrigation** to clear the discharge, and topical fluoroquinolones (e.g., moxifloxacin) to protect the cornea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-old man presents to the clinic with a 6-day history of redness, burning, and a gritty foreign body sensation in the right eye that has recently spread to the left eye. He reports a mild sore throat and clear rhinorrhea over the past week. On physical examination, there is bilateral conjunctival injection, watery discharge, and prominent follicles on the palpebral conjunctiva. A tender, palpable lymph node is noted in the ipsilateral preauricular region. Visual acuity is 20/20 in both eyes, and the pupillary response is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the next step in management?', '["Bacterial conjunctivitis; topical erythromycin ophthalmic ointment", "Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis; urgent referral for topical corticosteroid therapy", "Allergic conjunctivitis; topical olopatadine antihistamine/mast cell stabilizer drops", "Viral conjunctivitis; supportive care with cold compresses and artificial tears", "Herpes simplex keratitis; topical ganciclovir ophthalmic gel"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This presentation is classic for **viral conjunctivitis**, most commonly caused by **adenovirus** (frequently associated with a recent viral upper respiratory tract infection or pharyngoconjunctival fever). Hallmark clinical features include: (1) acute onset of watery/serous discharge, (2) burning or grittiness, (3) progression from unilateral to bilateral involvement, (4) conjunctival injection with palpebral follicles, and (5) a tender **preauricular lymphadenopathy**. The condition is highly contagious but self-limiting. Management is strictly **supportive** with cold compresses, artificial tears, and hand hygiene. Topical antibiotics are not indicated unless a secondary bacterial infection is suspected.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-old gentleman presents with a 2-day history of bilateral eye redness accompanied by intense, intolerable itching and a stringy, mucoid discharge. He notes that these symptoms are worse during the spring season and are accompanied by frequent sneezing. Physical examination reveals bilateral conjunctival injection, marked chemosis (conjunctival edema), and a cobblestone-like appearance of the papillae on the superior palpebral conjunctiva. Visual acuity is normal, and there is no preauricular lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy?', '["Urgent surgical debridement of the superior palpebral conjunctiva", "Oral acyclovir antiviral therapy", "Topical olopatadine (dual-acting antihistamine and mast cell stabilizer)", "Topical ciprofloxacin (fluoroquinolone antibiotic drops)", "Topical prednisolone acetate (corticosteroid drops)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The hallmark of **allergic conjunctivitis** is bilateral, intense **pruritus** (itching). Other classic findings include a history of atopy (sneezing, seasonal allergies), stringy or mucoid discharge, conjunctival injection, chemosis, and **cobblestone papillae** on the superior tarsal/palpebral conjunctiva. First-line management involves allergen avoidance and topical dual-acting agents like **olopatadine** or ketotifen, which provide both immediate antihistamine relief and long-term mast cell stabilization. Topical steroids are reserved for severe, refractory cases under specialist guidance due to risks of glaucoma and cataracts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 53-old male who wears extended-wear contact lenses presents with a 2-day history of rapid-onset redness, pain, and thick, yellowish-green purulent discharge in the left eye. He reports that the eyelids were completely ''glued shut'' in the morning. Physical examination reveals conjunctival injection, a normal pupillary response, and a small, hazy focal corneal infiltrate on fluorescein staining. Visual acuity is 20/80. What is the most likely pathogen, and what is the most appropriate empiric therapy?', '["Adenovirus; supportive care with cold compresses", "Staphylococcus aureus; topical erythromycin ointment", "Neisseria gonorrhoeae; intravenous ceftriaxone and topical saline irrigation", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa; topical fluoroquinolone drops (e.g., moxifloxacin or ciprofloxacin)", "Streptococcus pneumoniae; topical sulfacetamide drops"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acute bacterial conjunctivitis typically presents with a unilateral or bilateral red eye, thick purulent discharge, and morning crusting (''eyelids glued shut''). In patients who wear **contact lenses**, the bacterial profile shifts significantly towards Gram-negative rods, particularly **Pseudomonas aeruginosa**, which can cause rapid, aggressive corneal melting and perforation. Therefore, empiric therapy in any contact lens wearer must cover Pseudomonas. Topical **fluoroquinolones** (such as ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, or moxifloxacin) are the preferred first-line agents. Topical erythromycin or bacitracin-polymyxin is insufficient for contact lens wearers due to inadequate Gram-negative coverage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 33-old woman with a history of recurrent lower back stiffness presents with a 3-day history of severe right eye pain, redness, and marked photophobia. She reports that looking at light triggers a deep, aching pain in the eye, and even shining a light in the contralateral eye triggers pain in the affected eye. Physical examination reveals a circular pattern of conjunctival injection localized around the cornea (ciliary flush), a constricted pupil measuring 2 mm (compared to 4 mm in the unaffected eye) with sluggish response, and inflammatory cells and flare in the anterior chamber on slit-lamp examination. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the initial management?', '["Herpes simplex dendritic keratitis; topical trifluridine or ganciclovir", "Bacterial keratitis; topical hourly fluoroquinolone drops", "Scleritis; systemic NSAIDs or systemic immunosuppressants", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma; topical pilocarpine and intravenous acetazolamide", "Anterior uveitis (iritis); topical corticosteroids (prednisolone) and topical cycloplegics (homatropine)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This clinical presentation is highly characteristic of **anterior uveitis (iritis)**, characterized by: (1) deep aching pain, (2) intense **photophobia** (direct and reactive/consensual, caused by pupillary constriction), (3) **ciliary flush** (circumcorneal injection), (4) a constricted/miotic, sluggishly reactive pupil, and (5) **cells and flare** (white blood cells and proteins suspended in the aqueous humor due to blood-aqueous barrier breakdown) on slit-lamp exam. It is strongly associated with HLA-B27 seronegative spondyloarthropathies (e.g., ankylosing spondylitis). Management involves topical **corticosteroids** (e.g., prednisolone acetate) to reduce inflammation, and topical **cycloplegics/mydriatics** (e.g., homatropine, cyclopentolate) to dilate the pupil, relieve pain from ciliary spasm, and prevent posterior synechiae (adhesions between iris and lens).'),
