    (v_topic_id, 'A 61-old male presents to the clinic with a 5-day history of redness, burning, and a gritty foreign body sensation in the right eye that has recently spread to the left eye. He reports a mild sore throat and clear rhinorrhea over the past week. On physical examination, there is bilateral conjunctival injection, watery discharge, and prominent follicles on the palpebral conjunctiva. A tender, palpable lymph node is noted in the left preauricular region. Visual acuity is 20/20 in both eyes, and the pupillary response is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the next step in management?', '["Herpes simplex keratitis; topical ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Bacterial conjunctivitis; topical erythromycin ophthalmic ointment", "Allergic conjunctivitis; topical olopatadine antihistamine/mast cell stabilizer drops", "Viral conjunctivitis; supportive care with cold compresses and artificial tears", "Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis; urgent referral for topical corticosteroid therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This presentation is classic for **viral conjunctivitis**, most commonly caused by **adenovirus** (frequently associated with a recent viral upper respiratory tract infection or pharyngoconjunctival fever). Hallmark clinical features include: (1) acute onset of watery/serous discharge, (2) burning or grittiness, (3) progression from unilateral to bilateral involvement, (4) conjunctival injection with palpebral follicles, and (5) a tender **preauricular lymphadenopathy**. The condition is highly contagious but self-limiting. Management is strictly **supportive** with cold compresses, artificial tears, and hand hygiene. Topical antibiotics are not indicated unless a secondary bacterial infection is suspected.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 63-old lady presents with a 4-day history of bilateral eye redness accompanied by intense, intolerable itching and a stringy, mucoid discharge. She notes that these symptoms are worse during the spring season and are accompanied by frequent sneezing. Physical examination reveals bilateral conjunctival injection, marked chemosis (conjunctival edema), and a cobblestone-like appearance of the papillae on the superior palpebral conjunctiva. Visual acuity is normal, and there is no preauricular lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy?', '["Topical olopatadine (dual-acting antihistamine and mast cell stabilizer)", "Urgent surgical debridement of the superior palpebral conjunctiva", "Topical ciprofloxacin (fluoroquinolone antibiotic drops)", "Oral acyclovir antiviral therapy", "Topical prednisolone acetate (corticosteroid drops)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The hallmark of **allergic conjunctivitis** is bilateral, intense **pruritus** (itching). Other classic findings include a history of atopy (sneezing, seasonal allergies), stringy or mucoid discharge, conjunctival injection, chemosis, and **cobblestone papillae** on the superior tarsal/palpebral conjunctiva. First-line management involves allergen avoidance and topical dual-acting agents like **olopatadine** or ketotifen, which provide both immediate antihistamine relief and long-term mast cell stabilization. Topical steroids are reserved for severe, refractory cases under specialist guidance due to risks of glaucoma and cataracts.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-old gentleman who wears extended-wear contact lenses presents with a 4-day history of rapid-onset redness, pain, and thick, yellowish-green purulent discharge in the right eye. He reports that the eyelids were completely ''glued shut'' in the morning. Physical examination reveals conjunctival injection, a normal pupillary response, and a small, hazy focal corneal infiltrate on fluorescein staining. Visual acuity is 20/100. What is the most likely pathogen, and what is the most appropriate empiric therapy?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae; topical sulfacetamide drops", "Neisseria gonorrhoeae; intravenous ceftriaxone and topical saline irrigation", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa; topical fluoroquinolone drops (e.g., moxifloxacin or ciprofloxacin)", "Adenovirus; supportive care with cold compresses", "Staphylococcus aureus; topical erythromycin ointment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acute bacterial conjunctivitis typically presents with a unilateral or bilateral red eye, thick purulent discharge, and morning crusting (''eyelids glued shut''). In patients who wear **contact lenses**, the bacterial profile shifts significantly towards Gram-negative rods, particularly **Pseudomonas aeruginosa**, which can cause rapid, aggressive corneal melting and perforation. Therefore, empiric therapy in any contact lens wearer must cover Pseudomonas. Topical **fluoroquinolones** (such as ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, or moxifloxacin) are the preferred first-line agents. Topical erythromycin or bacitracin-polymyxin is insufficient for contact lens wearers due to inadequate Gram-negative coverage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-old lady with a history of recurrent lower back stiffness presents with a 2-day history of severe left eye pain, redness, and marked photophobia. She reports that looking at light triggers a deep, aching pain in the eye, and even shining a light in the contralateral eye triggers pain in the affected eye. Physical examination reveals a circular pattern of conjunctival injection localized around the cornea (ciliary flush), a constricted pupil measuring 3 mm (compared to 5 mm in the unaffected eye) with sluggish response, and inflammatory cells and flare in the anterior chamber on slit-lamp examination. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the initial management?', '["Acute angle-closure glaucoma; topical pilocarpine and intravenous acetazolamide", "Bacterial keratitis; topical hourly fluoroquinolone drops", "Herpes simplex dendritic keratitis; topical trifluridine or ganciclovir", "Anterior uveitis (iritis); topical corticosteroids (prednisolone) and topical cycloplegics (homatropine)", "Scleritis; systemic NSAIDs or systemic immunosuppressants"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This clinical presentation is highly characteristic of **anterior uveitis (iritis)**, characterized by: (1) deep aching pain, (2) intense **photophobia** (direct and reactive/consensual, caused by pupillary constriction), (3) **ciliary flush** (circumcorneal injection), (4) a constricted/miotic, sluggishly reactive pupil, and (5) **cells and flare** (white blood cells and proteins suspended in the aqueous humor due to blood-aqueous barrier breakdown) on slit-lamp exam. It is strongly associated with HLA-B27 seronegative spondyloarthropathies (e.g., ankylosing spondylitis). Management involves topical **corticosteroids** (e.g., prednisolone acetate) to reduce inflammation, and topical **cycloplegics/mydriatics** (e.g., homatropine, cyclopentolate) to dilate the pupil, relieve pain from ciliary spasm, and prevent posterior synechiae (adhesions between iris and lens).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 61-old man presents with a 2-day history of unilateral left eye redness, moderate pain, photophobia, and a foreign body sensation. Physical examination reveals conjunctival injection and mild eyelid swelling. Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein staining demonstrates a clear, branching **dendritic corneal ulcer** with prominent terminal bulbs. Visual acuity is slightly decreased. Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacotherapy, and which is strictly contraindicated?', '["Topical ciprofloxacin drops are indicated; topical cycloplegics are strictly contraindicated", "Topical ganciclovir gel or oral acyclovir is indicated; topical corticosteroids are strictly contraindicated", "Topical natamycin drops are indicated; systemic acyclovir is strictly contraindicated", "Immediate corneal patch grafting is indicated; topical antivirals are strictly contraindicated", "Topical prednisolone acetate is indicated; topical ganciclovir gel is strictly contraindicated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The presence of a **dendritic corneal ulcer** with terminal bulbs on fluorescein staining is pathognomonic for **Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) epithelial keratitis**. Management consists of topical antivirals (e.g., **ganciclovir gel**, trifluridine drops) or oral acyclovir/valacyclovir. **Topical corticosteroids are strictly contraindicated** in epithelial HSV keratitis, as they suppress local immune responses, leading to rapid viral replication, enlargement of the ulcer into a geographic configuration, and significantly increased risk of corneal melting, scarring, and perforation.'),
